  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1126 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nA sharp upper level trough over Arizona is moving steadily to the\neast. Thunderstorms are common across southern Arizona into\nnorthern Sonora, ahead of the main vort max moving across the\nnorthern Gulf of California. Diffluent flow over southern New\nMexico and a weak shortwave trough has lead to increasing\nconvective shower coverage over south-central New Mexico, and more\nrecently around El Paso. This activity is expected to intensify \nas it spreads northeast through the afternoon hours.\n\nNear-term models have been fairly inconsistent with the details\nthrough the evening and overnight hours. It seems the current\nprecip will be the main wave of precip, with a secondary area of\nshowers and thunderstorms possibly moving into southern Dona Ana\nand El Paso Counties closer to sunset. Activity over Arizona looks\nto become weaker and more scattered as it shifts into southern New\nMexico overnight, with some CAMS really inhibiting precip coverage\nlater tonight. But, it seems wise to hold onto shower chances as\nlong as the main trough axis is to our west. Also, the GFS has\nmost of the area in the left exit region of a 100+ knot jet streak\novernight, further favoring rain chances.\n\nSome small hail could accompany stronger embedded thunderstorms\nthis afternoon and evening, particularly over Otero and Hudspeth\nCounties.\n\nSnow levels will drop to around 9500-10,000 feet overnight, with\nlight snow accumulations possible in the highest elevations of \nSacramento Mountains, though mostly around uninhabited high peaks. \n\nA northern stream shortwave trough will roll across northern New\nMexico on Saturday. Continued cyclonic flow and cooler air aloft\nwill promote increasing cloud cover and convective shower chances\nover northern sections of the CWA Saturday afternoon. Scattered\nrain and \"graupelly\" snow showers will be possible over the\nSacramento Mountains and the Black Range around mid to late \nafternoon, though no accumulation is expected. Elsewhere, expect\nbreezy west winds across the lowlands, with some gusts around 35\nmph at times. \n\nLighter NW winds will prevail on Sunday, with more sunshine, no\nshowers, and temperatures remaining about 3 to 6 degrees below \nnormal.",
